\title{Separability, Floors and Ceilings for the Capped Trailing-Zero Dial\\[2mm]
\large An exact tie-attenuation theory for rank-correlation diagnostics on binary key spaces}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give an exact theory of the rank-correlation capacity of the \emph{capped
trailing-zero statistic} $T_u(x)=\min(v_2(x),u)$ on $b$-bit key spaces, where
$v_2$ is the $2$-adic valuation. Rank correlation against a tied predictor is
bounded above by a \emph{tie ceiling} determined entirely by the predictor's tie
profile; we compute this ceiling in closed form and derive its structural
consequences. Our main result is a \emph{separation law}: for $1\le u\le b$,
$\rho^2(b,u)=\tfrac67(1-8^{-u})(1+\tfrac1{4^b-1})$, so the two design knobs
enter as independent multiplicative factors and the whole
$\text{bitlen}\times\text{cap}$ ceiling table is rank one,
$\rho^2(b,u)\rho^2(b',u')=\rho^2(b,u')\rho^2(b',u)$. We prove strict
monotonicity in each knob and that changing the bit length moves the ceiling by
less than $2\cdot4^{-b}$, uniformly in the cap. We then prove a
\emph{distribution-free floor law}: if no value of a tied statistic carries more
than a fraction $a<1$ of the sample, its ceiling satisfies $\rho^2>1-a^2$; in
particular a \emph{balanced} statistic has $\rho^2>3/4$, i.e.\ $\rho>0.866$,
independently of arithmetic, bit length, cap and draw law. Sharpness is
witnessed by the profile $[15,1]$ with $\rho^2=3/17$, localising the failure of
a $0.53$ floor to modal mass in $[0.848,0.8955]$, the upper end witnessed by the
two-block profile $[8955,1045]$. A \emph{coarsening law} shows
that merging tie classes never raises the ceiling and that lowering the cap by
one is exactly such a merge; the resulting \emph{gap law} bounds the
tie-resolution advantage of $T_u$ over bare parity below by $3/32$ in $\rho^2$
and above by $0.07$ in $\rho$, with supremum $\sqrt{6/7}-\sqrt{3/4}=0.059795\ldots$.
Consequently an empirically recorded advantage of $0.10$--$0.15$ cannot be a
granularity artefact and forces the parity reading at least $0.03$ below its own
ceiling. A final section treats balanced (fixed Hamming weight) key laws, whose
binomial profile inherits the floor through the modal fraction identity
$b\binom{b-1}{w-1}=w\binom{b}{w}$, is pinned from both sides at $w=b/2$, and
sits within $0.07$ in $\rho$ of the uniform ceiling.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The diagnostic setting}

A recurrent pattern in the empirical study of arithmetic algorithms is the
\emph{dial}: a coarse integer-valued summary of an input, correlated against a
continuous response (a running time, an acceptance rate, a success frequency).
The dial studied here is the trailing-zero count of a key,
$v_2(x)=\max\{k:2^k\mid x\}$, capped at a design parameter $u$:
\[
T_u(x)=\min\bigl(v_2(x),u\bigr),\qquad x\in\{0,1,\dots,2^b-1\}.
\]
Capping is universal in practice, since deeper valuations are exponentially
rare, and it supplies the second knob. The design knobs are therefore the
\emph{bit length} $b$ and the \emph{cap} $u$ (the resolution). Setting $u=1$
degenerates the dial to bare parity, the \emph{bare count}.

The empirical observation under analysis is a robustness claim recorded across a
two-dimensional sweep of $(b,u)$: at every cell the Spearman rank correlation
between $T_u$ and the response was at least $0.53$, and $T_u$ outperformed the
bare count by $0.10$--$0.15$; no cell exhibited a breakdown. This paper replaces
that finite sweep with exact mathematics, organised around two questions.
\emph{Capacity}: how high can the correlation read at a given cell, given that
a tied predictor cannot reach $|\rho|=1$? \emph{Attribution}: which recorded
effects are explained by capacity, and which exceed what capacity could
manufacture?

\subsection{Summary of contributions}
\begin{itemize}
\item \S\ref{sec:sep}: the separation law
